I have a radiative transfer code, the input in N level atmospheric
profiles (N between 75-100) of temperature, humidity, ozone,
co2,ch4,n2o concentration, surface temperature, surface temperature,
view angle, plus surface emissivity.  Output is M radiances per
profile (M between 500-2500). Can you help me build a DNN to predict
the radiance,given these inputs

Good problem — this is essentially a fast neural emulator for a
hyperspectral RT model (the kind of thing used to speed up
RTTOV/CRTM/LBLRTM-type forward models for OSSEs or retrieval
systems). A few design decisions matter a lot here before you write
any Keras/PyTorch code, so let me walk through them and then give you
a working skeleton.

>>>

Key design decisions

1. Fixed input grid. Your profiles have variable N (75–100 levels),
but a DNN needs a fixed-size input. Interpolate every profile onto one
reference pressure grid (e.g. 100 levels spanning your full pressure
range) before training. Don't zero-pad — that introduces
discontinuities the network has to learn around. Pressure-log
interpolation (linear in ln(p)) is standard.

2. Feature transforms.:

  Temperature: use as-is (z-score normalized per level).

  Humidity, O3, CO2, CH4, N2O: these are trace-gas concentrations that
  span orders of magnitude and are close to log-normal — log-transform
  them before normalizing. This alone often improves accuracy more
  than architecture changes.
  
  Scalars: surface temperature (z-score), view angle → use as
  secant/cosine (airmass factor) rather than raw degrees, since
  transmittance depends on airmass, not angle linearly.

  Surface emissivity: if this is a single broadband value, treat as a
  scalar. If it's spectrally resolved (one value per output channel,
  which is common for M~500-2500 IR channels), it's actually a
  per-channel input feature — that changes the architecture (see
  below). I'll assume scalar/band-averaged for the skeleton, but flag
  where to change it if it's spectral.

3. Output compression — do this, it matters a lot. With M between 500
and 2500, radiances across adjacent channels are highly correlated
(they sample overlapping/broad absorption features). Don't predict all
M outputs directly from a dense layer — instead:

  Run PCA/EOF decomposition on your training radiance spectra, keep
  ~20-50 components (typically captures >99.9% of variance for
  hyperspectral IR).
  
  Train the network to predict the PCA coefficients (small output
  layer, easier optimization, much less overfitting).
  
  Reconstruct the M radiances with the fixed (or fine-tuned) linear PCA decoder.

This is exactly how operational NN emulators for RTTOV/CRTM are built,
and it's the single biggest lever for both accuracy and speed here.

4. Work in brightness temperature, not radiance. Radiance is a
nonlinear (Planck) function of temperature and spans large dynamic
range across channels (window vs. absorption bands). Converting to
brightness temperature makes the target smoother and more linear in
the inputs, which the network fits more easily. Convert back to
radiance via inverse Planck for final output if you need physical
units.

5. Architecture. A profile is a vertical sequence, so a small 1D-CNN
(or even just a well-normalized MLP) over the stacked gas/temperature
channels captures local vertical structure (e.g. a CO2 layer's effect
depends on neighboring levels) better than a flat MLP treating each
level independently. Concatenate the profile encoder output with the
scalar branch, then a dense trunk predicting PCA coefficients.

