A few things worth flagging before you plug in real data:

  Surface emissivity: I treated it as one scalar. If it's actually
  spectral (a value per output channel), it should be concatenated as
  an extra channel-aligned input to the decoder stage, not the encoder
  — otherwise the network has to learn to re-derive per-channel
  structure it's already been handed. Tell me if that's the case and
  I'll restructure it.
  
  PCA fit: fit the PCA/ProfileNormalizer only on the training split,
  never on val/test, or you'll leak information.

  Decoder fine-tuning: start with finetune_decoder=False
  (physically-grounded PCA reconstruction) to get a working baseline,
  then try True once the pipeline is validated — it usually buys a bit
  more accuracy at the cost of interpretability.

  Data volume: for M~1000-2500 channels and reasonable generalization
  across atmospheric states, expect to need on the order of tens of
  thousands to a few hundred thousand training profiles, covering your
  full range of latitude/season/surface type. If you're generating
  this synthetically from the RT code itself, use a Latin hypercube or
  similar design over your input space rather than pure random
  sampling — it covers the space more efficiently.

  Validation metric: don't just look at MSE on PCA coefficients —
  check reconstructed brightness-temperature error per channel (bias
  and RMSE) against your RT code's actual output, since PCA truncation
  error compounds with network error.

