I can;t interpolate because the different N corresponds to different surface proessures. So for ocean N = 97, for Mt Everest or Anttica N =75, for Dead Sea N = 100

Reconciled variable surface pressures through sigma-coordinate normalization approach
Reconciled variable surface pressures through sigma-coordinate normalization approach

Good catch — that's a real physical difference, not just a
data-formatting nuisance, and pure pressure-grid interpolation would
be wrong: Everest's atmosphere genuinely stops around 325 hPa, so
there's no real data to interpolate down to 1000 hPa, and any padding
would be fabricating atmosphere that doesn't exist.

The standard fix (this is exactly how NWP models like ECMWF/GFS handle
terrain-following levels) is to interpolate onto a sigma coordinate
instead of a pressure coordinate:

σ = p / p_surface

σ runs from 0 (top of atmosphere) to 1 (the surface) for every
profile, regardless of what the actual surface pressure is. So
Everest's profile and an ocean profile both map cleanly onto the same
fixed number of σ-levels (say 100), with σ=1 always meaning "the
surface," wherever that physically is.

Two things to add back in because σ alone throws away information:

  Surface pressure becomes an explicit scalar input. The same σ level
  corresponds to a different actual pressure depending on terrain —
  and pressure broadening of absorption lines depends on the real
  pressure, not σ. Without this the network can't tell "σ=0.5 over the
  ocean" from "σ=0.5 over Everest," which are physically very
  different atmospheric states.

  Interpolate in log(σ) near the top (thin, rapidly varying) and it's
  fine roughly linear lower down — same idea as before, just swap the
  coordinate.

Let me update the script.Now update where it's used and add p_surface as a scalar input.
Refined documentation and demonstrated interpolation for variable-pressure profile  sUpdated and re-checked — syntax is clean.

Summary of the change: profiles now interpolate onto a fixed sigma
grid (σ = p/p_surface, 0=TOA, 1=surface) instead of a fixed pressure
grid, so terrain of any elevation maps onto the same number of levels
without fabricating nonexistent atmosphere below high ground or
truncating real atmosphere over the ocean. surf_pressure is added as
an explicit scalar input, since two profiles at the same σ level can
sit at very different actual pressures (Everest vs. ocean), and
pressure broadening of the absorption lines depends on the real
pressure, not σ.

One more thing to watch for once you build the real training set:
class balance across terrain type. If your dataset is mostly ocean
profiles (N≈97) with only a handful of high-terrain cases (N≈75), the
network will underfit those — worth checking your sample distribution
over surface pressure/elevation and oversampling the extremes if
they're rare, since those are exactly the cases where the sigma-grid
physics matters most.

Similarly surface emissivity is also spectral
Architected compression strategy for spectral emissivity integration

Right — that changes the architecture meaningfully. Emissivity varies
per channel just like radiance does, so it can't be a single scalar
feeding a bottleneck the same way surface temperature does. But you
also can't hand the raw M-length emissivity vector to a small MLP
trunk without blowing up the parameter count.

The fix is the same trick as the output side: emissivity spectra are
themselves smooth/low-rank (this is true for both ocean models like
IREMIS and land spectral libraries like ASTER/CAMEL) — so PCA-compress
emissivity too, and feed those few coefficients in as inputs alongside
the other scalars, rather than as a single number.

