Also update the doc comment further down that references RadianceCompressor by old name (already renamed via class rename, but double check nothing else references it) and re-check compile

Clean — no stale references, syntax checks out.

What changed: RadianceCompressor is now a generic SpectralCompressor,
used twice — once fit on the target BT spectra (~20-50 components),
once fit on the emissivity spectra (likely fewer, ~5-10, since
emissivity curves tend to be smoother than absorption-line-dominated
radiance). The emissivity PCA coefficients get concatenated onto the
other scalars and flow into the same scalar branch — no change needed
to the trunk/decoder architecture itself, since it was already generic
on n_scalars.

Two things to double check against your actual data:

  Same channel grid. This only works cleanly if emissivity is defined
  on the same wavenumber grid as your output radiances (or can be
  interpolated onto it first). If your RT code takes emissivity on a
  coarser native grid (common — surface emissivity atlases are often
  lower resolution than the instrument channels), interpolate it onto
  the M-channel grid before fitting the PCA, otherwise the compressed
  coefficients won't align with anything physically.

  Ocean vs. land emissivity behavior differs a lot. Ocean emissivity
  is a smooth, physically-modeled function of view angle and wind
  speed;land/desert/ice emissivity spectra have sharper features
  (e.g. quartz reststrahlen bands in desert sand). If your dataset
  spans both, check that a single global PCA basis captures both
  regimes well — if land spectra are systematically poorly
  reconstructed, it's worth fitting separate emissivity compressors
  per surface type, or just increasing n_emis_pca.

