The upper atmosphere tempearure and WV profiles are very similar, of
course ozone has varivbility. So would compressing the (100 sigma)
level T,Q,etc be beneficial

Weighed PCA compression benefits against radiative transfer
sensitivity trade-offsWeighed PCA compression benefits against
radiative transfer sensitivity trade-offsYes — and this is actually
the standard approach in the retrieval/NWP literature for exactly the
reason you're describing: the atmosphere's vertical structure has far
fewer effective degrees of freedom than the number of levels you
sample it on.

Why it works, physically:

  Temperature is tightly constrained by hydrostatic balance and
  radiative-dynamical coupling — the profile shape doesn't vary
  independently level-by-level, it varies in a handful of large-scale
  vertical modes (lapse-rate perturbations, tropopause height shifts,
  boundary-layer inversions). Above the tropopause especially, T and Q
  variability collapses to almost nothing, exactly as you said.
  
  Water vapor is more variable than T but still highly structured —
  most of its variance loads onto a similarly small number of modes,
  with somewhat more needed to capture boundary-layer/moist-layer
  sharpness.
  
  Ozone has real independent variability — column amount and the
  altitude of the peak concentration both vary substantially (tropics
  vs. midlatitude vs. polar vortex), so it needs more modes to
  represent faithfully.

  CO2, CH4, N2O are nearly well-mixed gases. Their vertical shape has
  very little independent variability — it's mostly a magnitude
  scaling plus a small stratospheric aging gradient. These might not
  need more than 2-5 components each, or arguably don't need a learned
  encoder at all — a couple of physically motivated scalars might
  suffice.

This is the same logic operational physical retrieval algorithms use
(AIRS, IASI physical retrievals project the state vector onto ~10-20
eigenvectors for T/Q rather than retrieving level-by-level) — you're
not doing anything unusual here, you're following well-trodden ground.

Practical benefits for your network specifically:

Cuts input dimensionality from ~600 (100 levels × 6 vars) down to
maybe 60-100 total coefficients — far fewer parameters, faster
training, less overfitting risk, and less training data needed to
constrain it well.

It also means you can likely drop the CNN encoder entirely and replace
it with a plain MLP on the concatenated EOF coefficients — the
convolution's job (learning vertical correlation structure) is already
done by the PCA. Simpler architecture, easier to debug, probably just
as accurate.

Denoises: unphysical high-frequency wiggles across levels (which the
network would otherwise have to learn to ignore) get smoothed out by
construction.

Caveats to check before committing:

Don't pick a fixed number of components a priori — fit PCA on your
training profiles and look at cumulative explained variance per
variable; keep enough to hit something like 99.9-99.99%, then verify
by checking level-by-level reconstruction error against a physically
meaningful tolerance (e.g. <0.1-0.2 K for T, a few % relative for Q,
tighter for O3 near the peak since that's radiatively
important). Quick way to check:

python code

pca = PCA().fit(temperature_profiles)  # (n_samples, n_levels)
cum_var = np.cumsum(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)
n_needed = np.searchsorted(cum_var, 0.9999) + 1

PCA is linear — sharp features like a strong boundary-layer inversion
or a moist layer edge can need more components than the smooth bulk of
the profile does, since they're localized and don't compress as
well. Check reconstruction error specifically in those regions, not
just the global average.

Fit the PCA on a training set that actually spans your full
climatology (tropics through polar, including the ozone hole if that's
in scope) — a narrow training distribution will give you a compact
basis that fails on the tails.

Do this per variable, separately (T, Q, O3, and probably a
lighter/shared treatment for the well-mixed gases) rather than one
joint PCA across all 6 stacked — the physical variability scales and
structures are too different to share a basis well.

Want me to fold this into the script — replacing the CNN profile
encoder with per-variable EOF compression feeding a simpler MLP trunk?

I did not notice that you put in a CNN into the DNN?

Yes — it's in the ProfileEncoder class, specifically these lines from the script I gave you:

class ProfileEncoder(nn.Module):
    """1D-CNN over the vertical dimension. Input (batch, n_vars, n_levels)."""

    def __init__(self, n_vars, n_levels, out_dim=128):
        super().__init__()
        self.net = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Conv1d(n_vars, 32, kernel_size=5, padding=2),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Conv1d(32, 64, kernel_size=5, padding=2),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.AdaptiveAvgPool1d(1),  # global pool over levels
        )
        self.flatten_proj = nn.Linear(64 * 1, out_dim)

This takes your stacked (T, Q, O3, CO2, CH4, N2O) profiles as a
6-channel, 100-length sequence and runs two 1D convolutions across the
vertical dimension before feeding into the trunk. The reasoning at the
time was that a convolution can pick up local vertical correlation
structure (e.g. a layer's absorption depends on its neighbors) better
than a flat MLP that treats each level as independent.

Given what you just pointed out — that T and Q vertical structure is
dominated by a handful of large-scale modes, and that EOF/PCA
compression is the natural way to represent that — the CNN is now
doing redundant work. The PCA basis already captures the vertical
correlation structure explicitly and more efficiently than asking a
CNN to discover it implicitly from data. So my suggestion is to
actually remove the CNN and replace ProfileEncoder with a plain MLP
operating on the concatenated per-variable EOF coefficients — simpler,
fewer parameters, easier to interpret, and likely just as accurate or
better given limited training data.

Want me to make that edit — swap out the CNN encoder for per-variable
PCA compression feeding a plain MLP?

