A couple of things that might help while you're working through it,
given the Matlab background:

  numpy arrays and PCA via sklearn.decomposition.PCA map over fairly
  directly from Matlab's matrix/pca() mental model — the main
  adjustment is usually just axis-order conventions (numpy defaults to
  samples-as-rows, same as Matlab, so that part's not too bad) and
  0-indexing.

  The PyTorch-specific pieces (nn.Module, the training loop, tensors
  vs. arrays) are the more genuinely new territory if you haven't used
  a DL framework before — worth testing those in isolation with toy
  data before wiring the whole pipeline together.

  If it's useful, you could prototype the PCA/EOF compression step and
  check the explained-variance/reconstruction-error numbers directly
  in Matlab first, since that part doesn't need any deep learning
  framework at all — then only bring the compressed coefficients into
  Python once you're confident in the compression itself.

