Check which you have — if scipy.io.loadmat throws an error like
NotImplementedError: Please use HDF reader for matlab v7.3 files,
that's your answer.  On structs vs. separate variables — this is the
real practical issue. If you save like this in Matlab:
  matlabsave('profiles.mat', 'T', 'Q', 'O3', 'CO2', 'CH4', 'N2O', 'surf_pressure', 'radiance')
then in Python:
import scipy.io as sio
data = sio.loadmat('profiles.mat')
T = data['T']          # straight into a numpy array, no fuss
Q = data['Q']
radiance = data['radiance']

Each variable comes back as a clean numpy array. If instead you have
everything nested in one struct (e.g. profile.T, profile.Q,
profile.radiance), loadmat wraps it in an awkward nested object
(mat_struct / structured array with dtype=object), and unpacking
nested structs/cell arrays reliably gets fiddly, especially with mixed
sizes like yours (variable N per profile). It's doable with
squeeze_me=True, struct_as_record=False options, but it's extra code
that breaks in subtle ways with cell arrays.

My recommendation: save with flat top-level variables from Matlab like
above, one array per physical quantity, and skip structs entirely for
this pipeline. If your data's already saved as structs and you can't
easily resave it, let me know and I can write the unpacking code —
just flagging it's more code and more fragile than the flat
approach.


