I'll have about 38000 test profilesradiances. Far fewer than the
millions I know many pople train on, but these test profiles are
chosen for variability.

38,000 is very likely enough for this problem, and the "millions"
comparison you're thinking of comes from a different regime of problem
than yours.

Why the comparison doesn't transfer directly: The people training on
millions of samples are usually doing image classification, language
modeling, or line-by-line spectroscopic databases — tasks where the
input space is combinatorially huge or the target function has fine,
high-frequency structure that only shows up with massive data. Your
problem is a smooth, physically constrained regression: temperature,
humidity, and trace-gas profiles map to radiance through well-behaved
(if nonlinear) radiative transfer physics, not through arbitrary pixel
combinations. Once you compress inputs to EOF coefficients (~10-20
each for T/Q, fewer for the well-mixed gases, more for O3) and outputs
to PCA coefficients (~20-50), you're really training a regression from
maybe 50-70 effective input dimensions to ~30 effective output
dimensions — not from 600 raw inputs to 2500 raw outputs. That's a
much friendlier problem, and 38k samples gives you a healthy number of
samples per effective dimension.

Precedent for this specific task: operational fast-RT regression
models (e.g. the classic diverse profile sets used to train RTTOV's
regression coefficients) have historically used sample sizes in the
range of tens to a couple thousand carefully chosen diverse profiles,
not millions — precisely because they exploit the same smoothness and
low intrinsic dimensionality you're relying on. Modern NN-based RT
emulators in the literature typically train on tens of thousands to
~100k profiles. 38k sitting at the lower end of that range, but
selected deliberately for variability rather than randomly, likely
punches above its raw count — random climatological sampling wastes a
lot of samples clustered near the mean state; a diversity-selected set
doesn't.

Things worth doing given this sample size specifically:

  Use k-fold cross-validation rather than a single train/val/test
  split, or at least a few different random splits, to get a stable
  estimate of generalization error. With 38k you can afford 5-fold CV
  without it being computationally painful, and it protects you from
  an unlucky single split — especially important since the set was
  deliberately curated rather than i.i.d. sampled.
  
  Split by profile "family," not just randomly, if your diverse set
  has recognizable clusters (e.g. tropical ocean, polar, desert,
  mid-latitude storm). Random splitting risks near-duplicate profiles
  landing in both train and test, which would inflate your apparent
  accuracy. Stratify or group-split so similar profiles stay together.

  Fit a simple linear (or PCA-to-PCA linear) baseline first, before
  the DNN. Since BT (rather than raw radiance) is a fairly linear
  function of the inputs after your log-transforms, a plain linear
  regression from input EOFs to output PCA coefficients might get you
  surprisingly far, cheaply, with your full 38k. This tells you how
  much the nonlinearity of the DNN is actually buying you, and gives
  an early, very fast sanity check on the whole pipeline
  (normalization, PCA, everything) before you spend time tuning
  network architecture.

  Keep the network small relative to 38k samples. Given the
  reduced-dimension inputs/outputs, you likely don't need the 256-256
  trunk I sketched earlier — something more like 64-128 units per
  layer, with dropout or weight decay, will resist overfitting much
  better at this data scale. Watch the train/val loss gap closely as
  your main overfitting signal.

  You have an option most people training on fixed datasets don't: you
  own the RT code. If validation reveals the model is weak in a
  particular regime (e.g. high emissivity contrast, low-latitude ozone
  extremes), you can generate more profiles specifically there rather
  than being stuck with whatever you already have — a cheap form of
  active learning, since presumably running the forward RT model to
  generate more truth data is your bottleneck, not collecting real-world
  measurements.

Nothing here needs action from you today — just context to have in
mind while you're working through the rest at your own pace.

